From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 00:00:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vendor crash-handler 0.7.0 for riscv64 support

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [riscv64 support needs a newer transitive dependency, but crash-handler's own Cargo.toml pins its two resolved versions out of semver range of each other]

The "Build fbuild 2.5.21 manylinux_riscv64" job still fails after the
existing vendor-crash-context-riscv64.patch, with a *different* compile
error in a *different* crate:

  error[E0425]: cannot find type `__jmp_buf` in this scope
    --> crash-handler-0.6.3/src/linux/jmp.rs:51:16
  error[E0609]: no field `fpregs` on type `crash_context::mcontext_t`
    --> crash-handler-0.6.3/src/linux/state.rs:461:48

zccache (an external git dependency, not an fbuild workspace member)
pins `crash-handler = "0.6"`, which resolves to crash-handler 0.6.3.
That crate version has no riscv64 branch in its own linux/jmp.rs
(`__jmp_buf`) or linux/state.rs (reading `uc_mcontext.fpregs`/`__fpregs`
for the float state) -- this is crash-handler's *own* arch-specific code,
separate from (and not fixed by) vendoring crash-context. Cargo.lock
resolves a second, unrelated crash-handler 0.7.0 for running-process's
pin; that one already has riscv64 support (EmbarkStudios/crash-handling,
same 0.7.0 release that added crash-context's riscv64 getcontext()), so
only the 0.6.3 line used by zccache needs fixing.

crash-handler can't just be bumped to 0.7.0 for zccache's pin either:
zccache's own Cargo.toml requires `crash-handler = "0.6"`, a caret
requirement that rejects 0.7.0/0.8.x outright, and zccache is not ours
to patch.

Vendors the complete, unmodified crash-handler 0.7.0 release (from its
own published .crate tarball) under vendor/crash-handler/, with only its
Cargo.toml version string turned back to "0.6.3" so it keeps satisfying
every "crash-handler = 0.6" requirement in the graph, and wires it in via
a second [patch.crates-io] entry in the top-level Cargo.toml, alongside
the existing crash-context one. crash-handler 0.7.0 depends on
`crash-context = "0.6"`, which the existing crash-context patch already
resolves to the riscv64-capable vendored copy, so the two patches compose
correctly. No hand-written code: the riscv64 __jmp_buf layout, the
riscv64/s390x float-state branch in state.rs, and everything else come
straight from crash-handler's own tested, released 0.7.0 source.

Validated with `cargo check --target riscv64gc-unknown-linux-gnu`
(rust-std component installed) in three steps, each against a throwaway
crate depending on crash-handler the same way zccache does:
1. Plain registry crash-handler 0.6.3 (no patches): fails compiling
   crash-context, matching the error 0002 already fixes.
2. Registry crash-handler 0.6.3 + 0002's crash-context patch only (no
   this patch): reproduces the CI job's exact failure byte-for-byte --
   same E0425 on jmp.rs:51 (`__jmp_buf`) and the same two E0609s on
   state.rs:461-462 (`mcontext_t.fpregs`).
3. This patch's vendored crash-handler + 0002's crash-context patch
   together (i.e. how fbuild's own Cargo.toml resolves both): compiles
   cleanly for riscv64gc, no errors.
The patch applies cleanly on top of 0002-vendor-crash-context-riscv64.patch
against a fresh v2.5.21 checkout.

Signed-off-by: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
---
diff --git a/Cargo.toml b/Cargo.toml
index f4514cf..21a87d6 100644
--- a/Cargo.toml
+++ b/Cargo.toml
@@ -216,3 +216,14 @@ debug = false
 # requirement; everything else, including the riscv64 getcontext asm, is
 # unmodified upstream code.
 crash-context = { path = "vendor/crash-context" }
+
+# crash-handler 0.6.3 (zccache's pin) lacks riscv64 entirely in its own
+# linux/jmp.rs (`__jmp_buf`) and linux/state.rs (`uc_mcontext.fpregs`) --
+# a separate crate from crash-context above, and not fixed by the patch
+# above. running-process's crash-handler 0.7.0 pin already has riscv64
+# support, so only the 0.6.3 line needs it. Vendor the real upstream 0.7.0
+# release (EmbarkStudios/crash-handling, same riscv64 work) with its
+# version string turned back to "0.6.3" so it still satisfies zccache's
+# "0.6" requirement; it depends on `crash-context = "0.6"`, which the
+# patch above already resolves to the riscv64-capable vendored copy.
+crash-handler = { path = "vendor/crash-handler" }
